Both the not-signed-in branch (105) and any RPC/network failure branch (109) return the identical `null` — no error object, code, or reason is threaded through in either case.

The caller compounds this: components/LogFoodSheet.tsx:255-263 (attachFoodRef) wraps the call in try/catch and collapses even a thrown exception to `ref = null` with no console output at all — contrast this with the commit-rejection path a few lines below (line 350: `console.warn("[log] commit rejected", ...)`), which at least logs. The resolveFoodRef failure path has zero observability anywhere.

The UI then renders the collapsed null as one fixed message regardless of cause: components/LogFoodSheet.tsx:541-542
  541  canCommit={grams > 0 && foodRefId !== null}
  542  hint={foodRefId === null ? "Sign in to log from the catalog." : null}

I confirmed no test exercises resolveFoodRef at all: lib/log-client.test.ts only covers snapshotFor, liveStrip, buildCommitRequest, and commitLogEntry (grep for describe/it blocks shows no resolveFoodRef suite), so this is not a case where an existing guard merely has a hole — there is no guard.

Scenario is concrete and realistic for this app: Sasha is signed in on her phone, searches the catalog, taps a food; pickCatalogRow -> attachFoodRef -> resolveFoodRef fires the RPC over a cellular connection. A timeout, dropped connection, or transient Postgres/RLS error sets `error`, resolveFoodRef returns null exactly as the logged-out path would, and the sheet shows 'Sign in to log from the catalog.' with Save disabled — even though she is authenticated and the real cause was a failed network call. There is no toast, no retry button, and no log line anywhere a developer or Sasha could see to know the RPC (not her session) failed.

This does not cause data loss or wrong numbers (canCommit gates the entry before any commit/log write), and it is recoverable by backing out and re-picking the food (which re-invokes resolveFoodRef and may succeed once the transient condition clears) — so it does not rise to 'sync loss' or 'wrong numbers shown'. It is best characterized as an edge-case, degraded-UX bug with a genuinely misleading message and no diagnostic trail, i.e. the claimed 'medium' severity is the right fit rather than an under- or over-call.

